+-21   The depth images in both the simulation and the real robot are acquired from a 480 × 640 sensor
+-22   resolution. Then, the depth image is processed and resized to 48 × 64 resolution. The proprioception
+-23   is sent by the motor system through Cyclone DDS at 500Hz. We run the visual encoder process
+-24   at 10Hz and the rest of the parkour network at 50Hz. The vision encoder process acquires a depth
+-25   image, computes the image latent, and sends the latent vector to the parkour network using ROS
+-26   message. The parkour network sends joint position targets to the motors with different kp and
+-27   kd factors on each joint, shown in Table 6. The motors generate torque through their built-in PD
+-28   controller. To ensure safety, we clipped the joints target position based on the torque limit τmax :
+-29   clip (q target , (kd ∗ q̇ − τmax )/kp + q, (kd ∗ q̇ + τmax )/kp + q)
+-30   We use the Unitree H1 humanoid robot for our real-world test, which is equipped with an Intel
+-31   RealSense D435i and an Intel Core i7 12-core NUC onboard. The robot has 19 joints including the
+-32   arm and waist. We assign different kp, kd parameters for each joint as shown in Table 6. We use
+-33   ROS2 and Cyclone DDS for the communication between the policy and motors. The depth encoder
+-34   sends its embedding to the recurrent network at 10Hz. The recurrent network receives and sends the
