+-B    Robot Encoding vs No Robot Encoding
+-The main paper presents five cross-robot training settings involving various combinations of flat-
+-terrain and stair-climbing data. We provide extended analysis for Setting 1 (flat data from B2;
+-stair data from Aliengo and Stoch3), comparing models trained with explicit robot encodings under
+-otherwise identical conditions. Table 5 shows the stair-climbing results using policies trained with
+-explicit robot encodings.
+-We use robot encodings as part of observation input to the model, derived from predefined metadata
+-as shown in the table 2. However, we observe that explicit robot encoding does not lead to reliable
+-generalization. Specifically, robots like Go1 and Lite3, whose embeddings are significantly different
+-from those seen during training, fail to exhibit meaningful behavior. These robots attempt to walk but
+-immediately collapse with erratic actions, indicating poor transfer to out-of-distribution embeddings.
